<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Chickasaw Nation</h1><p class="st-meta">1700 CE – 1907 CE (approx.) · capital Tishomingo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A formidable south-eastern nation, allies of the British and enemies of the French, who after removal built a self-governing republic in Indian Territory.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Chickasaw descended from Mississippian chiefdoms of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>; the Spanish of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernando-de-soto">Hernando de Soto's</a> expedition met them, as Chicaza, in 1540 to 1541, and were attacked in their winter camp. By around 1700 they lived in a cluster of towns near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupelo-mississippi">Tupelo</a> and had become key partners of English traders from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-carolina">Carolina</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Towns were led by hereditary chiefs, the minko, and war leaders, within matrilineal clans. Chickasaw warriors raided neighbours for captives sold to the English and traded deerskins for guns and cloth. They defeated <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-louisiana">French Louisiana</a> and its allies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ackia">Ackia</a> in 1736. Under leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piomingo">Piomingo</a> they allied with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> after 1783, and some families became planters owning enslaved Africans.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-pontotoc-creek">Treaty of Pontotoc Creek</a> of 1832 the Chickasaw sold their homeland and moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a> in 1837. They adopted a constitution in 1856 with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tishomingo-oklahoma">Tishomingo</a>. Allotment ended their government in 1907, but the Chickasaw Nation has since been revived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/chickasaw-nation.jpg" alt="Chickasaw Nation" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Muriel Wright [1] · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Chickasaw_Nation.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1700 CE – 1907 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tishomingo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Piomingo, Tishomingo, Cyrus Harris</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chickasaw traditional religion, later Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chickasaw</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chickasaw, Chikasha</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1804&amp;focus=chickasaw-nation" aria-label="Chickasaw Nation on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2227 919 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chickasaw Nation lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2547 1175L2570 1008"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2570 1008h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2547 1175h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2547 1175h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1804&amp;focus=chickasaw-nation">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
